Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026
Notes: Period live-action drama spanning 1990s Milwaukee and early-2000s Seattle.

FADE IN:

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - DAY

A worn fifth-floor Milwaukee apartment. NARRATOR, a lean young man with guarded eyes and plain work clothes, sorts MA'S pill bottles while she dozes beneath an afghan.

(SOUND: a game show murmurs from the television, pill bottles click against the table)

The door opens. NICK enters first as always -- powerfully built, reddish-brown hair uncombed, scars on his chin and left eyebrow, brown suede jacket over work clothes.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Big brother was mine. Nick was the guy everybody was scared of. When he walked into a bar or gym, people took notice, and they moved out of the way.

NICK
What's Ma doin' asleep at this time of day?

NARRATOR
I don't know. Why?

NICK
You got to keep an eye on her. That's how old people go downhill. Pretty soon they don't want to go outside, eat, or do nothing.

MA wakes and stares uncertainly at her sons.

MA
Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta there?

NARRATOR
Ma, we haven't been to church for fifteen years.

Her glassy eyes fail to recognize him. Then awareness returns.

MA
Oh, right. I am so sorry. I don't know what got into me.

Nick fixes NARRATOR with a hard, accusing stare.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Dad left when I was eight. Nick became my dad, and I stayed with Ma. Somebody had to take care of her.

NICK
You don't need a degree to get a good job. You just need to know somebody.

NARRATOR
Could you get me into the metal factory?

NICK
What kind of shit would you take at school? You're not gonna be a doctor or lawyer. Hell, you're too old anyway.

NARRATOR lowers his eyes and returns to the pills.

EXT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

NARRATOR chains his bicycle outside the neighborhood pizza parlor. SIONNA, a slender woman with short brown hair, pale skin, light-blue eyes, jeans, blue tennis shoes, and a fish-symbol necklace, trips on the pavement and brushes his shoulder.

(SOUND: bicycle chain rattles, traffic hisses over damp pavement)

NARRATOR
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

She brushes off her black V-neck shirt. They look at each other, momentarily speechless.

NARRATOR
I work here. What's your name?

SIONNA
Sionna.

NARRATOR
That's different. What does it mean?

SIONNA
My parents named me for an Irish goddess associated with the River Shannon.

NARRATOR watches her luminous eyes instead of listening.

NARRATOR
Do you want to come in and have some pizza?

SIONNA
No thanks. I don't eat pizza. But if you want to have coffee sometime, you can call me.

She writes her number and hands it to him.

SIONNA walks away, then looks back over her shoulder. NARRATOR remains rooted beside his bicycle.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was like I knew her. Like I had seen her somewhere before. She was intense, and I wanted the tide to cover me completely.

INT. MILWAUKEE PUBLIC LIBRARY - NIGHT

Old hanging lights illuminate endless shelves and century-old wooden floors. NARRATOR enters from the cold, still carrying Sionna's phone number.

(SOUND: radiator pipes knock, pages turn, distant footsteps cross the wood floor)

A LIBRARIAN with silver hair and a navy cardigan watches him approach the desk.

NARRATOR
How do I take a book home from here?

LIBRARIAN
Do you have a library card?

NARRATOR
No. How do I get one?

She slides him an application. Moments later, he walks the shelves and pulls out WUTHERING HEIGHTS.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was like I could get anything in there for free. Who needed school when everything was right there?

He checks out the book, hiding a pleased smile.

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

Students crowd a warm coffee shop with backpacks and textbooks. SIONNA, now in a loose green hooded top, waits at a small rear table while NARRATOR approaches clutching WUTHERING HEIGHTS.

(SOUND: milk steams, cups clatter, low student conversation fills the room)

SIONNA
What are you reading right now?

NARRATOR
Wuthering Heights. It's about a guy and girl who grew apart but were really in love.

SIONNA
The environment is like its people -- wild and unpredictable. Heathcliff's love never changes, but in the end that kind of love destroys him.

NARRATOR
A love that never changes will destroy you.

SIONNA
If it cannot change, yes.

NARRATOR considers this, seeing Nick and Ma in a new light.

SIONNA
Have you ever thought about going back to school?

NARRATOR
Nick says college is just nerds talking to hear themselves talk.

SIONNA
What do you say?

For the first time, NARRATOR has no borrowed answer.

EXT. FOREST CLEARING - DAY - DREAM

Sunlight scatters through ancient oaks. NARRATOR, dressed in a white linen shirt, red embroidered tunic, and brown hose, rides behind SIONNA on a dappled mare.

(SOUND: hooves press through sorrel, leaves whisper overhead)

They enter a meadow. NARRATOR helps SIONNA down and kisses her.

(MUSIC: warm strings swell beneath the stillness)

Her light-blue eyes hold him as if across centuries.

NARRATOR
Hold!

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NARRATOR'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

NARRATOR bolts upright in his narrow bed, sweating. WUTHERING HEIGHTS lies open beside him.

(SOUND: his breath pounds in the dark, a radiator clanks once)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It felt like my life for real. The girl was Sionna, but in another place, wearing old clothes. Make-believe. It had to be.

INT. YOGA STUDIO - DAY

Purple mats cover the floor. NARRATOR struggles through downward dog while his T-shirt falls over his face. SIONNA, supple and calm in dark exercise clothes, practices beside him.

(SOUND: measured breathing, bare feet shift against rubber mats)

NARRATOR collapses into corpse pose, exhausted but peaceful.

SIONNA
You were really great at that.

NARRATOR
Are you kidding me?

SIONNA
Your practice is your practice. The only thing that matters is what happens on your mat.

NARRATOR
My practice is not the best practice.

SIONNA
That's why they call it practice.

They smile.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Afterward, making pizzas didn't bother me. Customers didn't bother me. Nothing did. I was just calm.

EXT. KIRK STREET SIDEWALK - DAY

NARRATOR and SIONNA walk beneath green trees. Teenagers laugh near a hardware store, but SIONNA continues without reacting.

(SOUND: skateboard wheels scrape concrete, leaves rustle in a mild breeze)

NARRATOR
Doesn't that piss you off?

SIONNA
Why let other people bother you? Only when we become masters of ourselves can we really deal with the world.

They stop. NARRATOR kisses her.

SIONNA
Love is the universal language, and we know how to speak it.

NARRATOR
You're the only one I can speak it to.

SIONNA
We have to learn to speak it to everyone, even people we don't particularly like.

NARRATOR
That's going to be hard.

SIONNA
I could never love anyone like I love you.

NARRATOR
I could never love anyone the way I love you either.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

MA sits beneath her afghan while SIONNA arranges medication in a blue weekly container. NICK looms over NARRATOR, his scarred face red with anger.

(SOUND: plastic compartments snap shut, the television drones softly)

NICK
Ma missed her doctor's appointment. Where the hell were you?

NARRATOR
I forgot. I was with someone.

NICK
With who?

NARRATOR
I met a girl. We had a couple dates.

NICK
Don't you think Ma deserves better? Try looking at your priorities, Bro.

NARRATOR
I said I was sorry.

Later, NICK examines the organized pills and smirks.

NICK
How did you come up with this little idea?

NARRATOR
Sionna helped me.

NICK
This chick sure has you wrapped around her finger.

NARRATOR
Stop it, Nick. You haven't even met her.

NICK suddenly slaps the side of NARRATOR'S head.

(SOUND: the blow cracks sharply, NARRATOR'S ear rings)

NICK
Don't you ever talk that way about my mother.

He storms out and slams the door.

(SOUND: the doorframe shudders, MA whimpers in the bedroom)

NARRATOR touches his ear, fear turning slowly into anger.

INT. JOE'S BAR AND GRILL - NIGHT

Smoke hangs over a packed bar and pool room. NICK, still massive in his brown jacket, sees his former girlfriend LINDSEY beside her dark-haired boyfriend, MARCO.

(SOUND: pool balls crack, bottles clink, loud hard rock pounds from the speakers)

NICK
Who's this asshole?

LINDSEY
Nick, why does it matter? You broke up with me.

MARCO puts an arm around her.

MARCO
You got a problem with me?

Nick's fists clench. NARRATOR steps directly between them.

NARRATOR
You don't know this guy. Lindsey can see whoever she wants. Leave it, man.

NICK
You're next if you don't get out of my face.

NARRATOR
Stop it! No one cares about your ego, Nick!

Nick's fist swings toward him. NARRATOR jerks backward as JOE, a broad bar owner in a stained white shirt, grabs Nick.

(SOUND: chairs topple, patrons shout, a cue stick strikes the floor)

Nick head-butts JOE and tears free.

NARRATOR runs.

EXT. MILWAUKEE SIDEWALK - NIGHT

NARRATOR races through the cold, vaulting a trash can and dodging pedestrians.

(SOUND: his shoes slap pavement, breath rasps, distant shouting fades behind him)

He runs past the turn toward home and continues toward Sionna's duplex.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I knew what I was afraid of. Being alone. Taking responsibility for myself without Nick to back me up.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

A modest room with a green suede sofa, books, candles, and a gray cat. NARRATOR lies with his head in SIONNA'S lap while she massages his scalp.

(SOUND: rain taps the windows, the cat purrs near the sofa)

SIONNA
Your brother tried to hit you.

NARRATOR
He cares about me. I can't explain Nick completely.

SIONNA
No one who cares about you does that. You're showing him you don't need him.

NARRATOR
What's my purpose?

SIONNA
To find the true self within you and honor it. To be the best person you can while you're here.

NARRATOR
The first time I met you, it was like I had finally come home.

SIONNA
Me too, honey. Me too.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - EVENING

Months later. Schoolbooks and a used computer now belong to NARRATOR. MA eats peach pie while SIONNA, in a flowered blouse and silver bracelets, clears the plates.

(SOUND: forks scrape china, a police show plays quietly on television)

NICK enters in his brown suede jacket and kisses MA'S cheek.

MA
Oh, Nick! I thought we would never see you again.

SIONNA returns and offers her hand.

SIONNA
I'm Sionna. You must be Nick. Nice to meet you.

NICK reluctantly shakes it.

NICK
I hear you're a university lady.

SIONNA
That's right.

NICK
Sometimes all that school doesn't pay off. It's even harder for a woman to teach at that level.

SIONNA
More women attend college now than men. Education can only improve when more women bring their experience to it.

Nick stares, outmatched.

NARRATOR
I'm going to community college too. Business.

NICK
And who's watching Ma while you're screwing around with pencil-pushers?

NARRATOR
I will. Classes aren't all day. This was my decision.

Nick rises, kisses MA, then grips NARRATOR'S shirt near the door.

NICK
Take care of Ma.

NARRATOR
You know I will.

EXT. WERNER AND LOWE INTERSECTION - NIGHT

Nick's burnt-orange 1980 Pontiac Trans Am, yellow flames on its sides and eagle on its hood, carries NICK and MA through the intersection.

(SOUND: the V8 engine growls, a church bell fades behind them)

Headlights burst from the side.

(SOUND: tires shriek, metal explodes against metal)

The Pontiac spins into a pole. Glass rains over Nick's reddish-brown hair. MA hangs motionless in her seat.

(MUSIC: a low sustained note rises, then cuts to silence)

INT. MILWAUKEE POLICE STATION - DAY

NARRATOR sits before OFFICER MIKE DUPUIS, a large gray-haired officer with a thin mustache. A Styrofoam cup trembles in NARRATOR'S hands.

(SOUND: telephones ring, typewriters strike, distant cell doors clang)

OFFICER MIKE DUPUIS
They were hit broadside at Werner and Lowe. Your brother is in serious condition. Your mother passed away at the scene.

The cup slips from NARRATOR'S fingers and spills across the floor.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

The apartment is dark and empty. MA'S chair, afghan, and blue pill container remain exactly where she left them.

(SOUND: the wall clock ticks with oppressive clarity)

NARRATOR sinks beside the chair and clutches the afghan.

NARRATOR
Die, you fucking jackass. Die.

His cry collapses into a child's sob. SIONNA enters, kneels, and holds him.

(MUSIC: restrained piano notes emerge beneath his sobbing)

SIONNA
Let it out. We'll figure it all out.

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - DAY

Five days later. NICK lies bruised and diminished, oxygen under his nose, his right leg immobilized and a chest tube entering his side.

(SOUND: a heart monitor beeps, oxygen whispers through tubing)

NICK
Hey, Bro. I guess I really fucked up. Can't believe Ma's gone.

Tears streak his battered face.

NARRATOR
The mass was nice. She's at peace now.

NICK
I had three Jack and Cokes before I remembered church. The cops say vehicular manslaughter. It was a mistake. I almost died too.

NARRATOR
Whose fault was it, Nick?

NICK
You think I meant to kill her? You son of a bitch.

NARRATOR finally sees that Nick cannot rise, strike, or command him.

NARRATOR
Think about it, Nick.

He turns and leaves.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

Several months later. SIONNA opens her front door. NICK stands outside, thinner after his injuries but still imposing, hair disordered and scars stark against his pale face.

(SOUND: winter wind moans around the porch, the door hinges creak)

NICK
Can I talk to you?

SIONNA steps aside. Nick enters and towers over her.

NICK
If you hadn't messed with my brother, he'd have taken care of Ma. She'd be alive, and I wouldn't be going to jail.

SIONNA
Your choices have nothing to do with me. Sit down and we can talk rationally.

NICK drives her backward into the hallway wall.

NICK
You get away from my brother. Then you find money for my lawyers.

SIONNA
Nick, calm down.

He grips her throat and opens a switchblade.

(SOUND: the blade snaps open, SIONNA'S breath becomes a strangled squeak)

NICK
Break it off tonight, or this blade goes into your neck.

The front door crashes open. NARRATOR enters and seizes Nick's arm.

NARRATOR
Holy shit, Nick! What are you doing?

They struggle. The blade plunges beneath Sionna's left collarbone.

(SOUND: SIONNA gasps, the knife strikes bone, the cat bolts across the floor)

Nick elbows NARRATOR and runs. SIONNA slides down the wall, blood soaking her shirt.

NARRATOR catches her and presses both hands over the wound.

NARRATOR
Stay with me. Stay with me!

(SOUND: emergency sirens grow rapidly closer)

PARAMEDIC HARRIS, a compact woman in a dark uniform, rushes in with equipment and kneels beside Sionna.

PARAMEDIC HARRIS
Keep pressure there. Do not lift your hands.

NARRATOR
My brother stabbed her. Nick Olson stabbed her.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - LIVING ROOM - DAY

Months later, winter light crosses the room. SIONNA sits in a rocking chair, thinner and hollow-eyed, her left shoulder scarred beneath a loose shirt.

(SOUND: the chair creaks, a heater exhales warm air)

NARRATOR crouches before her.

NARRATOR
You know I love you, right? You are safe. I will protect you with my life.

SIONNA
I can't feel me. I can't feel myself anymore.

NARRATOR
Stay here. We can work things out.

SIONNA
As long as there is doubt, I can't go on with you. I need to go to my parents and see a therapist.

She walks toward the bedroom. NARRATOR remains beside the empty chair.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick was sentenced for the stabbing and the drunk-driving offense that killed Ma. Sionna lived, but the inner wound drove her away.

INT. SEATTLE APARTMENT - NIGHT

Seven years later. Rain streaks high windows above Seattle. The older NARRATOR, now broad-shouldered with neatly cut hair and a dark business shirt, sits beside his orange-and-white cat, BOZO.

(SOUND: steady rain, distant ferry horn, BOZO purrs)

Invoices marked TONY'S BOY'S PIZZA cover the table beside a laptop. A dozen store locations glow on a map.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Thanks to Tony, I did pretty well. I built my own chain in the Northwest. But seven years passed with hatred for my brother still inside me.

NARRATOR closes his eyes.

A vision: a radiant green road, an Indian SAGE in a white wrap, and a discarded pizza box.

SAGE
Stop judging things. Things are neither good nor bad. This is an illusion. Start living the truth.

The SAGE and pizza box vanish.

NARRATOR opens his eyes, smiling through tears. He reaches for his laptop.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Holding resentment kept me in my own prison. I finally had the key.

He books a flight to Milwaukee.

INT. GREEN BAY CORRECTIONAL INSTITUTION - VISITATION ROOM - DAY

Thick Plexiglas separates two gray booths. NARRATOR sits in a fitted dark coat, composed and successful. NICK enters in prison orange, older, scrawny, gray at the temples, his scars and weary black eyes still recognizable.

(SOUND: barred doors lock, telephones echo through the visitation room)

They lift their receivers.

NICK
Hi, Bro. Glad you came.

NARRATOR
Hi, Nick. We don't have much time, so I need you to listen.

Nick nods.

NARRATOR
You're an asshole who killed my mother, held me down, and tried to kill the love of my life. Because of you, I'll never see her again.

Nick lowers his eyes.

NARRATOR
But I forgive you. It sets me free from anger, regret, and loss. It releases your control over me forever.

Nick begins to cry openly.

NICK
You're right. I did all that shit. I'm so sorry. You're a big man, and I'm a small one. You deserve to live out there.

A prison guard gestures that time is nearly over.

NARRATOR
I still believe you can change. After all is said and done, I love you for being my brother.

NICK
I love you too. What happened with you?

NARRATOR
I own a pizza chain in Seattle. We're expanding. You always said I couldn't do it. I did it, Nick.

Nick smiles through his tears.

NICK
President Olson. That's fucking amazing. I'm proud of you, man.

The guard takes Nick by the arm.

NICK
Bye.

NARRATOR hangs up and watches his brother disappear behind the locked door.

(SOUND: the final steel door closes, leaving only the hum of fluorescent lights)

INT. MILWAUKEE AIRPORT - BOARDING GATE - DAY

NARRATOR stands among travelers with a boarding pass and a small bag. For the first time, his shoulders carry no visible weight.

(SOUND: boarding announcements echo, luggage wheels roll over tile)

The GATE ATTENDANT scans his pass and hands it back. His printed name reads PETER OLSON.

GATE ATTENDANT
Have a pleasant flight, Mr. Olson.

PETER looks toward the jet bridge.

NICK (V.O.)
President Olson.

PETER
That's me. President Peter Olson.

He walks forward into the light of the jet bridge.

PETER (V.O.)
This has been Nick and me.

FADE OUT.

THE END
